CONCLUSION
  HYP-3406's endpoint-owner support remains the live separator.
  Schwarz-Christoffel cut angles and Krasner local balls are promising
  compressions of owner support, but they must be tested against full
  endpoint labels; BDH/Mertens variance is useful as a residual ledger,
  not as a terminal scalar.  Bring/HLW/root-curve language is best used
  as a branch alarm: when a quotient hides ordered or degree-five payload,
  keep the whole PGF/root structure or name the debt.  The recursive
  CHARAL chain to try next is residue -> height/v2 -> owner support ->
  cut current -> local stability -> full root/branch payload.
